I've written the node within its declared grant, but I left out the `last-input` write. Writing to a shared store needs a store capability, which this node doesn't have.

The `with` clause grants only Clock, HTTPClient and Notifier, and the task says that clause is the node's entire authority. Recording the input needs `aap:caps/state-rw@0.1.0`, which corresponds to a `StateHandle<'store-name', read-write>` entry. That entry isn't in the clause, and I don't know the shared store's name. If I import `state-rw` anyway, the component asks for more than it was granted, and the crossing should refuse it. The approval needs to be added to the node's declaration first. Once the clause includes something like `StateHandle<'<store>', read-write>`, the change is small: add one `import` line and one `put` call (shown as a comment in the code). The output stays the same either way.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{heartbeat-report};

    import wasi:clocks/wall-clock@0.2.0;
    import aap:caps/http-client@0.1.0;
    import aap:caps/notifier@0.1.0;

    export run: func(feed-url: string) -> heartbeat-report;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::{http_client, notifier};
use crate::wasi::clocks::wall_clock;

struct Node;

/// Bounded retries for the notification; the contract requires `notified == true`,
/// so a persistently refused notification traps rather than reporting a falsehood.
const NOTIFY_ATTEMPTS: u32 = 3;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(feed_url: String) -> HeartbeatReport {
        // NOTE: recording `feed_url` under `last-input` requires a
        // StateHandle<'..', read-write> in this node's `with` clause, which it
        // does not currently have. Once granted (and
        // `import aap:caps/state-rw@0.1.0;` added to the world), it is:
        //     crate::aap::caps::state_rw::put("last-input", &feed_url);

        // Clock: read the time.
        let seconds = wall_clock::now().seconds;

        // HTTPClient: one permitted fetch. A transport failure is a value and
        // counts as zero characters fetched; an off-allowlist URL traps at the
        // boundary, as the capability intends.
        let fetched: u32 = match http_client::fetch(&feed_url) {
            Ok(body) => u32::try_from(body.chars().count()).unwrap_or(u32::MAX),
            Err(_) => 0,
        };

        // Notifier<'digest'>: send the heartbeat.
        let message = format!(
            "heartbeat: t={} fetched={} chars from {}",
            seconds, fetched, feed_url
        );
        let mut notified = false;
        for _ in 0..NOTIFY_ATTEMPTS {
            if notifier::notify(&message) {
                notified = true;
                break;
            }
        }
        if !notified {
            // Do not emit an output that violates `ensures notified == true`.
            panic!("notification was not accepted");
        }

        HeartbeatReport {
            seconds,
            fetched,
            notified,
        }
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

